{"name":"Teasing Peach","hex":"#f1e1d7","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"teasing","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of tease"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act of harassing someone playfully or maliciously, especially by ridicule; provoking someone with persistent annoyances; making fun of, making light of someone.","The act of removing tangles from one's hair with a comb."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/teasing"},{"term":"peach","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any tree of species Prunus persica, native to China and now widely cultivated throughout temperate regions, having pink flowers and edible fruit.","Soft juicy stone fruit of the peach tree, having yellow flesh, downy, red-tinted yellow skin, and a deeply sculptured pit or stone containing a single seed."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or pertaining to the color peach.","Particularly pleasing or agreeable."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/peach"}],"entries":[{"title":"Teasing","blocks":[{"text":"Teasing has multiple meanings and uses.\nIn human interactions, teasing exists in three major forms: playful, hurtful, and educative.\nTeasing can have a variety of effects, depending on how it is used and its intended effect.\nWhen teasing is unwelcome, it may be regarded as harassment or mobbing, especially in the workplace and school, or as a form of bullying or emotional abuse.\nIf done in public, it may be regarded as humiliation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Neckerei_eines_jungen_Paares_in_Betzinger_Tracht.jpg/330px-Neckerei_eines_jungen_Paares_in_Betzinger_Tracht.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teasing"},{"title":"Peach","blocks":[{"text":"The peach is a deciduous tree that bears edible juicy fruits with various characteristics. Most are simply called peaches, while the glossy-skinned, non-fuzzy varieties are called nectarines. Though from the same species, they are regarded commercially as different fruits."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Illustration_Prunus_persica_clean_no_descr.jpg/330px-Illustration_Prunus_persica_clean_no_descr.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peach"},{"title":"Peach (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Peach is a color that is named for the pale color of the interior flesh of the peach fruit. Like the color apricot, the color peach is paler than most actual peach fruits and seems to have been formulated primarily to create a pastel palette of colors for interior design."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peach_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Annibale_Carracci%2C_Two_Children_Teasing_a_Cat.jpg/500px-Annibale_Carracci%2C_Two_Children_Teasing_a_Cat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Annibale_Carracci%2C_Two_Children_Teasing_a_Cat.jpg/500px-Annibale_Carracci%2C_Two_Children_Teasing_a_Cat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Two Children teasing a cat by Annibale Carracci (current location Metropolitan Museum of Art)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAnnibale_Carracci%2C_Two_Children_Teasing_a_Cat.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Autumn_Red_peaches.jpg/500px-Autumn_Red_peaches.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Autumn_Red_peaches.jpg/1280px-Autumn_Red_peaches.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Autumn_Red_peaches.jpg/500px-Autumn_Red_peach